[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is conclusive. I have direct confirmation:

- **`spine_router_0_0`** (AS 65100): `neighbor 172.16.0.7 remote-as 65201` — pointing at `leaf_router_0_1` ✓
- **`spine_router_0_1`** (AS 65101): `neighbor 172.16.0.11 remote-as 65201` — pointing at `leaf_router_0_1` ✓
- **`leaf_router_0_0`** (healthy): has `neighbor 172.16.0.4 remote-as 65100` + `neighbor 172.16.0.8 remote-as 65101` ✓
- **`leaf_router_0_1`** (faulty): `router bgp 65201` block exists, `network 10.0.1.0/24` is present — but **zero `neighbor` statements** — all peering declarations were stripped.

Both spines are `Active` (initiating TCP but no response accepted) because `leaf_router_0_1` has no `neighbor` block at all, so it neither initiates nor accepts BGP sessions. Without those sessions, `10.0.1.0/24` is never advertised into the fabric, and the spines have no route back to `pc_0_1`. This matches the "Destination Net Unreachable" from `10.0.0.1` (leaf_router_0_0) for the reverse path too.

